{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Omega Navigation System.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Omega Navigation System.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"omega navigation system omega navigation system","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Omega Navigation System.md","fileName":"Omega Navigation System.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Omega Navigation System","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-21 00:56\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Pre-Satellite Navigation Systems]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Pre-Satellite Navigation Systems","gps.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Omega Navigation System.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Omega Navigation System.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"omega navigation system omega navigation system","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Omega Navigation System.md","fileName":"Omega Navigation System.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Omega Navigation System","heading":"Omega Navigation System","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Omega was a global, passive radio-navigation system built around eight synchronized very-low-frequency transmitters. Its long wavelengths propagated through the Earth-ionosphere waveguide, allowing continuous worldwide coverage with relatively few stations. A receiver compared signals to obtain a two-dimensional position, but accuracy was only about two kilometers and the remote, high-power transmitter network was expensive to maintain. Omega demonstrated global coverage while also exposing the limits of terrestrial infrastructure.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Pre-Satellite Navigation Systems","gps.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Omega Navigation System.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Omega Navigation System.md","sourceType":"markdown